          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you give me a meal plan?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You get meal frameworks built around your goals and your starting point, not a fixed daily menu. The difference matters: a menu works until the week you travel or a dinner runs late, and then it stops working entirely. A framework tells you how to build a meal in any situation, which is what survives contact with a real schedule."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is nutrition coaching included or is it extra?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Included in every tier at no additional cost, and it is the same coaching at every tier. Macro targets, meal frameworks, photo logging with full macro breakdowns, and the behavioural framework are identical whether you are on Essential or Peak. The only practical difference is that clients who train more often have more moments where nutrition comes up inside a session. Nothing is held back for higher tiers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How does the AI meal photo analysis work?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You photograph a plate in the OmniFit app and FitMetrics AI returns a full macro breakdown of it. You can add a short description to help the AI read what it is looking at, and save meals you eat regularly so a repeat lunch takes one tap rather than a fresh entry. It exists because the reason most people stop tracking is friction, not motivation. Weighing and logging every ingredient is sustainable for about ten days; photographing a plate takes two seconds, and the coaching conversation starts from what you actually ate."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What if I hate dieting and tracking?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Then the framework is built for you specifically. The awareness phase exists to establish a baseline, not to make tracking permanent. The end state is identification, where the decisions are automatic and you are not logging anything. Tracking is a diagnostic tool with a defined lifespan, not the product."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are you a dietitian?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Nemezio LopezPerez is a NASM Certified Nutrition Coach with Precision Nutrition Level 1 and Precision Nutrition's Nutrition for Metabolic Health certification. Those cover nutrition coaching for healthy adults. Medical nutrition therapy is a registered dietitian's scope of practice, and clients managing a chronic disease are referred rather than coached around it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will you put me on a crash diet to hit a deadline?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No, at any tier and for any reason. Aggressive restriction produces a number on a scale and loses muscle doing it, and the behaviour it teaches is the behaviour that put most people here in the first place. If you have an event and a short window, the honest answer is that the timeline does not suit this approach."
          }
